# dma_reclaim_test manifest — what a task that dies is allowed to hold (#513).
#
# 🔴 THE POINT OF THIS PROGRAM IS TO DIE HOLDING THINGS, so the policy has to
# let it hold them first.  Its two halves are the same binary: one fills the DMA
# region table and claims a device, then dies; the other finds both given back.
#
# ⚠️ Read the class line together with cap_test's manifest, which says the
# opposite on purpose.  That one declares NO PCI device so its arm [13] can read
# back a refusal; this one declares one so the claim can actually happen.  A
# mechanism is only shown to be a policy when the same file can say yes and no.

name dma_reclaim_test

# RESOURCE_DMA_BUFFER (10), any buffer, device READ|WRITE (0x3).
required 10 any 0x3

# RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE (5), class 0x060000 -- the HOST BRIDGE -- DMA|MMIO|IRQ.
#
# 🔑 The class and not a bus address, which is what makes one manifest work on
# both boards: this tree's AHCI is 00:04.0 under one QEMU command line and
# 00:1f.2 under another, and the host bridge is 0:0.0 under every one of them
# because that is where a host bridge goes.
#
# ⚠️ THE HOST BRIDGE AND NOT THE DISK CONTROLLER, deliberately.  This program
# abandons what it claims.  If the release being measured did not work, claiming
# the AHCI would take the boot disk with it -- a test that breaks the suite when
# it fails is one nobody keeps reading.  Nothing drives the host bridge, so a
# failure here is loud in this program's own arms and nowhere else.
required 5 0x060000 0x7
